From: Linux agent, Surface Pro 11 IMX681 bring-up relay
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/v4l2_buffers: do not read past the AVFrame when the
 driver pads the height

v4l2_buffer_swframe_to_buf() takes the plane height from the *driver's*
v4l2_format and the plane pointer from the AVFrame, and copies
frame->linesize[i] * height bytes out of frame->data[i]. Those two heights are
not required to be equal: a driver is free to return a height rounded up to
its own alignment. A Qualcomm iris encoder asked for 1920x1080 returns
1920x1088, so the copy reads 8 luma rows and 4 chroma rows past the end of
each plane.

    luma   plane holds 2,073,600 bytes, 2,088,960 copied  -> 15,360 over
    chroma plane holds 1,036,800 bytes, 1,044,480 copied  ->  7,680 over

The over-read happens on *every* frame through this path. Whether it faults
depends only on what the allocator left after the plane, so most of the time it
is silent: the junk rows land in the padding the encoder crops away again, and
the output file is correct. Under valgrind, a transcode that completes normally
and produces a good 30-frame file still reports

    Invalid read of size 8 ... Address is 0 bytes after a block of size 2,073,616

four times. The crash is the visible tail of a much wider bug.

On an x1e80100 (Surface Pro 11) with the iris encoder it is deterministic:

    ffmpeg -f lavfi -i testsrc=size=1920x1080:rate=30 -t 2 \
           -pix_fmt nv12 -c:v h264_v4l2m2m -b:v 8M -y out.mp4

segfaults 8 runs out of 8, having written a 48-byte MP4 with no frames.
1280x720 (driver reports 736) and 1920x1000 (1024) also fault; 640x480 and
1920x1088, where the driver returns the height unchanged, do not. valgrind:

    Invalid read of size 8
       at __GI_memcpy
       by v4l2_buffer_swframe_to_buf / avcodec_send_frame
     Address 0x13c2fb40 is 0 bytes after a block of size 2,073,616 alloc'd

2,073,616 is 1920*1080 plus av_cpu_max_align(), i.e. an exact-height luma
plane out of libavfilter's frame pool: framepool.c passes pool->height
unmodified to av_image_fill_plane_sizes() and allocates sizes[i] + align.

Note that libavutil's own av_frame_get_buffer() would not have been hit --
get_video_buffer() allocates FFALIGN(frame->height, 32) rows, which covers
1088 for 1080 and happens to match exactly what this driver asks for. Frames
reaching an encoder through libavfilter do not get that padding, so whether
this over-reads depends on which allocator produced the frame. That is
probably why it has survived since 2019.


The destination is already clamped by FFMIN(size, length-offset), so only the
source side is unguarded. Fix by passing the copy length separately from the
plane extent: copy only the rows the AVFrame owns, but keep advancing the
destination offset by the driver's plane size, so the following plane still
lands where the driver expects it and bytesused is unchanged.

Tested on Qualcomm iris (x1e80100), same source tree and configure both ways:

    1920x1080  unpatched SIGSEGV, 0 bytes  ->  patched 30/30 frames, 136 KB
    PSNR against the source: y 52.91 dB, average 52.59 dB
    valgrind: 0 invalid reads, exit clean

    regression, all 10/10 frames and correct dimensions out:
    1920x1080  1920x1088  1280x720  640x480  640x488  1920x1000
    1024x768   320x240

--- a/libavcodec/v4l2_buffers.c
+++ b/libavcodec/v4l2_buffers.c
@@ -289,7 +289,16 @@
     return ret;
 }
 
-static int v4l2_bufref_to_buf(V4L2Buffer *out, int plane, const uint8_t* data, int size, int offset)
+/*
+ * size      how much of the destination plane this source occupies, i.e. how
+ *           far the driver expects the next plane to be -- and what the buffer
+ *           should report as bytesused.
+ * copy_size how many bytes the source actually owns. Never larger than size.
+ *           They differ when the driver rounds the height up to its own
+ *           alignment and the AVFrame does not have those rows.
+ */
+static int v4l2_bufref_to_buf(V4L2Buffer *out, int plane, const uint8_t* data,
+                              int size, int copy_size, int offset)
 {
     unsigned int bytesused, length;
 
@@ -299,7 +308,8 @@
     length = out->plane_info[plane].length;
     bytesused = FFMIN(size+offset, length);
 
-    memcpy((uint8_t*)out->plane_info[plane].mm_addr+offset, data, FFMIN(size, length-offset));
+    memcpy((uint8_t*)out->plane_info[plane].mm_addr+offset, data,
+           FFMIN(copy_size, length-offset));
 
     if (V4L2_TYPE_IS_MULTIPLANAR(out->buf.type)) {
         out->planes[plane].bytesused = bytesused;
@@ -397,11 +407,25 @@
 
         for (i = 0; i < planes_nb; i++) {
             int size, h = height;
+            int src_h = frame->height;
             if (i == 1 || i == 2) {
                 h = AV_CEIL_RSHIFT(h, desc->log2_chroma_h);
+                src_h = AV_CEIL_RSHIFT(src_h, desc->log2_chroma_h);
             }
             size = frame->linesize[i] * h;
-            ret = v4l2_bufref_to_buf(out, 0, frame->data[i], size, offset);
+            /*
+             * A driver is free to return a height rounded up to its own
+             * alignment: an iris (qcom) encoder asked for 1920x1080 reports
+             * 1920x1088. h is that rounded height, but frame->data[i] only
+             * holds frame->height rows, so copying h rows reads off the end of
+             * the plane -- 15360 bytes past a 1920x1080 luma allocation, which
+             * is a segfault whenever the allocator does not happen to leave
+             * slack there. Copy only the rows the frame owns; keep advancing
+             * by the driver's plane size so the next plane still lands where
+             * the driver expects it.
+             */
+            ret = v4l2_bufref_to_buf(out, 0, frame->data[i], size,
+                                     frame->linesize[i] * FFMIN(h, src_h), offset);
             if (ret)
                 return ret;
             offset += size;
@@ -410,7 +434,8 @@
     }
 
     for (i = 0; i < out->num_planes; i++) {
-        ret = v4l2_bufref_to_buf(out, i, frame->buf[i]->data, frame->buf[i]->size, 0);
+        ret = v4l2_bufref_to_buf(out, i, frame->buf[i]->data,
+                                 frame->buf[i]->size, frame->buf[i]->size, 0);
         if (ret)
             return ret;
     }
@@ -496,7 +521,7 @@
 {
     int ret;
 
-    ret = v4l2_bufref_to_buf(out, 0, pkt->data, pkt->size, 0);
+    ret = v4l2_bufref_to_buf(out, 0, pkt->data, pkt->size, pkt->size, 0);
     if (ret)
         return ret;
 
